


:: day_2_1
<<nobr>>
<<set $day to 2>>
<<set $day_step to 1>>
<<set $hint to "Stream the morning away.">>
<</nobr>>
I wake up before the alarm.

That hasn't happened in months. Maybe longer. My body's internal clock has been set to "ignore all stimuli until forced" since approximately freshman year of college (which I attended for one semester before the financial aid fell through and I discovered that motivation is a finite resource). But today my eyes open at 9:42 AM   forty-eight minutes before the alarm I actually set last night, the one Kira told me to set, the one she specifically said should be real and not the kind I snooze for ninety minutes   and I'm just... awake.

The ceiling crack is there. Still growing. Still splitting the plaster like the building's skull is fracturing in slow motion. But this morning it looks less like a verdict and more like geography. Just a crack. Just a thing that exists in a room where I also exist, and today that room contains a plan.

Kira's picking me up at noon.

I lie on the mattress for three minutes, not because I'm tired but because the warmth of the comforter is the closest thing to a hug my life currently offers, and I want to be strategic about when I leave it. My phone is on the floor beside me. I pick it up. No new messages   it's early by my standards and Kira said she had morning yoga. I imagine her in some studio somewhere, a bright room with wooden floors and too many plants, bending into shapes that my spine would file a restraining order against. She probably wears one of those fitted tank tops. The kind that 

Okay. Stop. Get up.

[[Get Up|day_2_2]]

:: day_2_2
<<set $day_step to 2>>
The apartment is the same. Obviously. Apartments don't rearrange themselves overnight based on the occupant's mood, though this one seems to try   the light from the window is hitting differently today, catching dust motes in a way that looks almost cinematic rather than depressing. Or maybe that's just me projecting. Maybe when you have somewhere to be and someone to see, the particles in the air rearrange themselves into something you can tolerate.

I step over the Monster can I left on the floor last night. The microwave burrito wrapper is still on the counter. The second monitor's standby light glows softly, the dashboard from last night's stream still queued up behind the dark screen, ready to remind me of my failures the moment I jiggle the mouse. I don't jiggle the mouse.

First things first: stream. Even today. Especially today. Because if I don't stream, the day becomes entirely about Kira, and that's too much pressure on a lunch that is not a date (except that Marcus called it a date, and she said "good boy" with a smirk emoji, and I called click-through rates hot in my head approximately forty times before falling asleep, but it's not a date).

I shower. Really shower. Not the lukewarm speed-rinse I usually do   I wait for the hot water, all eight minutes of it, standing there like a patient idiot while the pipes clank and groan and eventually produce something approaching warmth. I use the bar soap on my hair and then   and then I actually dig under the bathroom sink, past the extra toilet paper roll and the bottle of drain cleaner I bought six months ago and never used, and find a travel-size bottle of shampoo. It's from a hotel. I've never stayed in a hotel. Marcus gave it to me as a joke for my birthday   a little bottle of Marriott shampoo with a note that said "for emergencies." I think this qualifies.

The shampoo smells like coconut and something vaguely chemical. I work it into my hair with more effort than I've applied to my physical appearance in recent memory. Rinse. My hair feels... different. Lighter. Less like it's been lacquered to my skull.

I get out. Towel off. I do something I almost never do.

I look in the mirror.

It's not a revelation. The face staring back is the same one that's always been there   pale, dark circles, acne along the jawline, thin lips, the nose with the wide bridge, the eyes that can't quite commit to making contact even with their own reflection. My hair is wet and pushed back from my forehead and without the greasy curtain falling over it, my face looks more exposed. More there. More seen, in a way that makes me want to pull the hair back down immediately.

But I don't. I just look.

Marcus told me to wear the grey shirt. The grey shirt is the softest of the three and also the one with the fewest visible stains. I pull it on. Same black joggers   I only own the one pair, and laundry isn't happening before noon. I look at the crushed sneakers by the door and experience a brief, futile wish for better shoes. The sneakers go on. The navy hoodie goes on over the grey shirt. I zip it halfway, which is the closest thing to a "look" I'm capable of assembling.

9:58 AM. I've got two hours before Kira arrives. Time to stream.

[[Stream|apartment_map]]

:: day_2_3
<<set $day_step to 3>>
<<set $viewers to 2>>
I open OBS. The ritual is the same   jiggle the mouse, wake the monitors, watch last night's stats materialize on the second screen like a ghost I can't exorcise. I close the stats tab without reading them again. I know what they say. They say zero.

Gunmetal Requiem loads. I queue into a match. The webcam activates and there I am   slightly less terrible than usual, the hair situation marginally improved by the Marriott shampoo, the grey shirt a fractional upgrade from the black one. In the thumbnail preview I still look like a guy streaming from a closet, but at least today the guy washed his hair.

EliPlays - Live
Viewers: 2

<<eli "Morning stream, what's up everybody. Ranked grind continues. We're getting Gold today, I can feel it.">>

I can't feel it. I feel nothing. The two viewers are almost certainly not human.

The first match loads. I'm trying. I'm actually, genuinely trying today, because there's a part of my brain   the stupid, hopeful, newly-shampooed part   that thinks maybe today will be different. Maybe the algorithm will bless me. Maybe someone will click in and stay. Maybe I'll clutch something incredible and it'll get clipped and go viral and by the time Kira picks me up at noon I'll have a story to tell her over ramen, a good one, the kind where she leans forward and her eyes get bright and she says 

I die eighteen seconds into round one. Peeked a corner. Headshot. Instant.

<<eli "...okay.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "lol you died in 18 seconds">>

<<eli "Hey, DarkSlayer. Thanks for stopping by.">>

[[Continue Playing|day_2_4]]

:: day_2_4
<<set $day_step to 4>>
<<set $viewers to 3>>
Viewers: 3

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "good morning loser">>

<<eli "DarkSlayer. You're here before noon. I'm touched.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "i set an alarm 😂">>

He set an alarm. My dedicated hater set an alarm to watch me fail. There's a poem in that, somewhere, if I weren't too depressed to write it.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "wait did u wash ur hair??">>

<<eli "What?">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "bro u look different. less greasy. whats the occasion">>

<<eli "There's no occasion. I just... showered.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "LMAO he showered everybody. historic moment. somebody clip this">>

Nobody clips it. There are three viewers and one of them is DarkSlayer and the other two are probably browser tabs left open by mistake.

I play through the match. Die four more times. Get two kills, one of which was an accident   I was reloading and the enemy walked into my crosshair at the exact wrong moment, like we'd choreographed it. The scoreboard is ugly. 2 and 5 in the first half.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "2 and 5. the eli special">>

<<eli "I'm warming up.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "youve been warming up for years now">>

[[Continue Playing|day_2_5]]

:: day_2_5
<<set $day_step to 5>>
<<set $viewers to 5>>
Viewers: 5

A spike. Two new people. I sit up.

<<chat "stream_viewer_88" "what rank is this?">>

<<eli "Silver 2! Working toward Gold. Welcome in.">>

<<chat "stream_viewer_88" "oh">>

That "oh" contains an entire review. One syllable that means "I was hoping for something better and I've found something worse." I can hear the mouse hovering over the X button.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "hes been silver since september lol">>

<<chat "stream_viewer_88" "yikes">>

[[Continue Playing|day_2_6]]

:: day_2_6
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 6>>
<<set $viewers to 4>>
<<set $donations to 1>>
<<set $money += 1>>
<</nobr>>
stream_viewer_88 has left the channel.

I take a breath. Push through the second half. Play less stupid   anchoring sites, using gadgets, actually checking corners instead of sprinting through them like a raccoon in a convenience store. I end the match 7 and 9. Not good. But better than the morning sessions usually go.

Match two. I queue again. A donation notification pops up on my dashboard   a sound I hear so rarely that it takes me a full second to recognize what it is. My heart does something embarrassing.

$1.00 from GhostPepper_: "silver isn't that bad bro keep grinding"

One dollar. One actual dollar from a real person who watched me play badly and felt enough... what? Pity? Solidarity? Whatever it is, it's a dollar more than zero and the number on my dashboard changes from $0.00 to $1.00 and I feel a flush of something that I'm ashamed to call gratitude because it's one dollar and I'm grateful for it and that says everything about where I am.

<<eli "GhostPepper, thank you for the donation! Appreciate you, man. Yeah, Silver's the grind but we're climbing.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "1 dollar lmaooo dont spend it all in one place">>

<<chat "GhostPepper_" "np bro. ive been there">>

[[Continue Playing|day_2_7]]

:: day_2_7
<<set $day_step to 7>>
<<set $viewers to 3>>
GhostPepper_ has left the channel.

Gone. A dollar and a kind word and then back into the void. The viewer count drops to 3.

I play another match. Then another. The clock on my second monitor reads 11:15. I'm running out of time before Kira comes. Part of me wants to keep streaming   the irrational part, the part that thinks maybe the next match will be the one, maybe the algorithm will turn, maybe lightning will strike. But the other part   the part that washed its hair with stolen hotel shampoo   knows that I need to end this, close OBS, and spend the remaining forty-five minutes doing something I've never done before a social engagement: preparing.

Match four ends. 6 and 7. Almost positive K/D. Almost respectable. Almost.

<<eli "Alright chat, wrapping up the morning session. Got some stuff to do today. Thanks for hanging out. DarkSlayer, thanks for the... consistent presence.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "where u going? u never go anywhere">>

<<eli "I have a life outside of streaming, DarkSlayer.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "since when">>

<<eli "Since today, apparently. See you guys later.">>
<<set $money += 3.50>>
I hit End Stream. Dashboard:

[[Continue|day_2_8]]

:: day_2_8
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 8>>
<<set $viewers to 0>>
<<set $donations to 0>>
<<set $right_sidebar_mode to 0>>
<</nobr>>
Duration: 1 hour 45 minutes
Peak Viewers: 5
Average Viewers: 3.1
New Followers: 0
Donations: $3.50 ($1.00 from GhostPepper_, $2.50 from two micro-donations I barely noticed)
Chat Messages: 22 (mostly DarkSlayer)

Three dollars and fifty cents. For almost two hours of performance. 

That's $1.94 per hour. There are child lemonade stands with better margins.

But it's not zero. For the first time in eleven days, the donation counter has a number in it that isn't zero, and I cling to that distinction the way a drowning person clings to debris   it's not a boat, it won't save me, but it's something to hold while the current pulls.

I close OBS. I have forty minutes.

<<set $hint to "Prepare for your lunch with Kira.">>
[[Prepare for your lunch|day_2_9]]

:: day_2_9
<<set $day_step to 9>>
I spend them badly.

I change my shirt. Then change back. Then change again. The grey shirt is fine. Marcus said the grey shirt. The grey shirt is the play. I pull at the collar, which has that slightly stretched-out thing happening from too many washes. I tuck it in. Untuck it. Tuck just the front. I look like a divorced man at a Chili's. I untuck everything.

I brush my teeth again, even though I brushed them after my shower. I check my hair in the phone's front camera because the bathroom mirror is too honest and the phone camera has a slight smoothing effect that makes me look 15% less like a police sketch. The hair is better. Actually better. The hotel shampoo gave it a softness that's unfamiliar, like it belongs to someone who takes care of themselves. I push it back. No   forward. No   to the side. It settles into a vaguely intentional mess that's the best I'm going to get.

I pick at the skin around my thumbnail. Catch myself. Stop. Start again. Stop again.

I check my phone. Nothing from Kira. It's 11:51. She said noon. She's probably still driving. Or finishing yoga. Or doing that thing she does where she sits in her car for two minutes before going anywhere, adjusting the rearview mirror and picking the right song for the drive   she told me about that habit once, in a late-night Discord call, and I filed it away in the growing archive of Kira Facts that I maintain like a librarian with an unhealthy fixation.

11:54. I pace. My apartment is not big enough for pacing   three steps from the desk to the kitchenette, three steps back   but I do it anyway, back and forth, wearing a groove in the hardwood that probably already exists from the previous tenant's anxieties.

11:57. My phone buzzes.

<<kira "leaving now! be there in like 8 min. be ready or i'm honking 😤">>

<<eli "im ready">>

I'm not ready. I will never be ready. But I'm standing in my apartment in a grey shirt with washed hair and brushed teeth and a rapidly deteriorating sense of calm, and that's as close to ready as I get.

11:58. I put on my sneakers. The crushed heels. I look at them and feel the specific despair of a man who knows his shoes are the first thing people notice and the last thing he can afford to replace.

12:00. I check the mirror on my phone one more time. The face is still mine. It hasn't gotten better in the last three minutes.

12:04. A horn honks outside. Short, playful   two quick beeps that somehow sound exactly like her.
<<set $hint to "Prepare for your lunch with Kira.">>
[[Go outside|day_2_10]]

:: day_2_10
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 10>>
<<set $hint to "Go outside and meet Kira.">>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/neighbourhood.jpeg">>
<<set $locationName to "Outside Eli's Apartment">>
<</nobr>>
I grab my keys. Lock the door. Take the stairs two at a time, which I haven't done since I moved in. The front door of the building sticks   you have to hip-check it while turning the knob, a move I've perfected through eight months of practice   and then I'm outside, in the January cold, and Kira's car is at the curb.

It's a used Honda Civic. Grey-blue, clean, a small dent on the rear bumper that she told me happened in a parking lot ("some asshole in an SUV, I was literally inside Target"). The windows are slightly fogged from the heater. I can see her through the driver's side   a shape, backlit, leaning across to wave.

I walk to the passenger door. Open it and the car smells exactly like I remembered   Japanese cherry blossom, from the clip-on air freshener on the vent, sweet and floral and so specifically her that for a half-second I just stand there in the open door like a malfunctioning NPC.

<<kira "Get in, weirdo, you're letting the cold in!">>

I get in. Close the door. The warmth of the car wraps around me and the cold falls away and Kira is right there, right there, eighteen inches to my left, and she looks 

She looks like Kira. Which is to say she looks like the person who has been living rent-free in my head for the better part of a year and a half. Black bob, blunt bangs, wire-frame glasses. She's wearing an oversized olive-green jacket over a black turtleneck, and her cheeks are flushed from the cold or the yoga or the heater, a soft pink that makes her look like she's been painted by someone who understands what warmth means. The pixel-heart tattoo peeks out from the cuff of her jacket as she grips the steering wheel. She's got small hoop earrings today   gold, catching the wan January light.

<<kira "Hi!">>

She says it with the energy of someone who's been looking forward to this, which immediately makes me suspicious of my own perception because nobody looks forward to seeing me.

<<eli "Hey.">>

I sound like I'm ordering at a drive-through. Cool. Great. Very smooth, Eli.

<<kira "You look... different.">>

<<eli "I showered.">>

<<kira "No, I mean   you look nice. Did you do something to your hair?">>

She noticed. She noticed the hair. The Marriott shampoo was worth it. Every second of those eight minutes waiting for hot water was worth it because Kira Nomura is looking at my hair like it's noteworthy and her mouth is doing a small smile and I am going to die in this Honda Civic.

<<eli "I, uh. Found some shampoo.">>

<<kira "Groundbreaking.">>

She grins. It's the grin   the one where the left side of her mouth pulls up slightly higher than the right, asymmetric and real, the one she does when she's teasing someone she likes. I've cataloged her smiles without meaning to. There's the stream smile (bright, performative, both eyes crinkled), the friend smile (easy, relaxed, teeth showing), the thinking smile (lips pressed together, one eyebrow up), and this one   the teasing grin. My favorite. The one that makes her look like she knows a secret.

<<kira "Seatbelt.">>

<<eli "Right.">>

I fumble with the seatbelt. The click sounds loud in the warm car. She puts it in drive and pulls away from the curb, and the motion tips me slightly toward her   centrifugal force, basic physics, nothing romantic about it   but I overcorrect and lean hard against the door like the car is a centrifuge and I'm being flung outward.

<<kira "You okay?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Fine. Good.">>

<<kira "You seem nervous.">>

<<eli "I'm not nervous.">>

I am incandescently nervous. My palms are sweating into the upholstery of her passenger seat. I can smell the cherry blossom and beneath it something else   something that's just her, shampoo or lotion or the particular chemistry of a person's skin, and it's doing things to my circulatory system that a cardiologist would find concerning.

<<kira "I found the ramen place on TikTok. This girl posted a video of their tonkotsu and it had like two million views. The broth is supposed to take eighteen hours.">>

<<eli "Eighteen hours of broth. That's commitment.">>

<<kira "Right? Like, I can barely commit to an eight-hour stream. These people are simmering bones for almost a full day.">>

<<eli "To be fair, the bones are doing most of the work.">>

She laughs. The laugh   the real one, the burst that she tries to suppress by pressing her lips together and it doesn't work, it pushes through like water through a crack, high and sudden and slightly embarrassed. Her glasses slip down her nose half a millimeter and she pushes them back with one finger while keeping the other hand on the wheel.

<<kira "The bones are doing most of the work. I'm using that. That's a stream title waiting to happen.">>

<<eli "Feel free. I'll accept a citation in the corner of the screen.">>

<<kira "I'll put 'joke by EliPlays' in font size 2 in the bottom right.">>

<<eli "That's more visibility than I usually get.">>

She glances at me. Quick   a darted look, there and gone, but in the space of it I see something shift behind her glasses. Concern? Recognition? She heard the self-deprecation under the joke. She always hears it. Kira has this radar for the real thing hiding inside the bit, and she never lets it pass unmarked.

<<kira "Hey. How was your stream this morning?">>

<<eli "I made three dollars and fifty cents.">>

<<kira "That's something!">>

<<eli "It's the price of a gas station sandwich.">>

<<kira "It's three-fifty more than zero. That's a real person who watched you and decided you were worth paying for.">>

<<eli "Or they fat-fingered the donate button.">>

<<kira "Eli.">>

<<eli "Sorry. Yeah. It was something. GhostPepper said 'silver isn't that bad bro keep grinding.' Very motivational.">>

<<kira "See? Someone believes in you.">>

<<eli "Someone who immediately left the stream after donating one dollar.">>

<<kira "Okay, but they still donated! You know what my first donation was? Fifty cents. From my mom. Using my own PayPal. I literally donated to myself to see if the system worked.">>

<<eli "That's...">>

<<kira "Pathetic? Yeah and now I ">>

She stops. Catches herself. I know what she was going to say and now I make thousands a month. She stopped because she's kind enough to realize that completing that sentence in the car of a guy who made three dollars and fifty cents today would be a specific kind of cruelty, even delivered as encouragement.

<<eli "You can say it. I know you're doing well.">>

<<kira "I just don't want to ">>

<<eli "Kira. I'm happy for you. Seriously. The fact that my career is a dumpster fire doesn't mean I can't be happy that yours isn't.">>

She's quiet for a second. Turns left onto the main road. The blinker clicks in the silence.

<<kira "You're a good person, Eli.">>

<<eli "I'm a broke person who's nice about it. There's a difference.">>

<<kira "No. You're a good person.">>

[[Change the topic|day_2_11]]

:: day_2_11
<<set $day_step to 11>>
She says it with a finality that suggests the topic is closed. Her eyes are on the road. Her jaw is set in that way it gets when she's decided something and won't be argued with. I've seen her do this on stream   a viewer will say something dismissive and she'll counter it once, firmly, and then move on. The conversation is over because she said so.

We drive. She turns the radio on   low, background level, some J-rock playlist she has connected through her phone. The bass line thrums under our silence. I look out the window at the January city   grey buildings, grey sky, grey people in grey coats   and I feel less grey than I have in months.

<<kira "Oh! I forgot to tell you. You know that figurine the viewer sent me?">>

<<eli "The chibi one? Yeah.">>

<<kira "I put it on my desk and chat went NUTS. They were like 'she kept it!' Like of course I kept it? Someone made that by hand? But they were so excited. I got like thirty subs just from having it on camera.">>

<<eli "Thirty subs from a figurine.">>

<<kira "Parasocial economy, baby. You'd be surprised what makes people feel invested.">>

<<eli "...you just called it 'parasocial economy.'">>

<<kira "I'm writing a college essay about it. Not for college. Just for fun. Is that weird?">>

<<eli "It's the least weird thing about you.">>

<<kira "What's the MOST weird thing about me?">>

<<eli "The way you eat Kit-Kats.">>

<<kira "I eat Kit-Kats normally!">>

<<eli "You bite straight across all four bars at once. That's psychopath behavior.">>

<<kira "It's EFFICIENT.">>

<<eli "It's a war crime.">>

She swats my arm without looking. Her hand connects with my bicep   brief, light, the casual physical contact of someone who's comfortable touching you   and I feel it like a static shock. A small, warm explosion that radiates from the point of impact outward, down my arm, across my chest. She doesn't notice. She's already pulling into a parking spot outside the ramen place, angling the Civic into a space that's too small for most cars but exactly right for hers.

<<kira "We're here!">>

[[Go inside|day_2_12]]

:: day_2_12
<<set $day_step to 12>>
The restaurant is called Noren. Small, narrow, maybe fifteen seats   a long wooden counter facing the open kitchen and a handful of two-tops along the opposite wall. The steam from the kitchen fogs the front windows. It smells like pork bone and garlic and something deep and savory that hits the back of the throat and activates a hunger I didn't know I had. My stomach growls loud enough that Kira hears it and laughs.

<<kira "When did you last eat?">>

<<eli "Define 'eat.'">>

<<kira "Consumed food that wasn't previously frozen.">>

<<eli "...Tuesday? Maybe?">>

<<kira "Eli, it's Thursday.">>

<<eli "Time is a construct.">>

<<kira "Nutrition is not.">>

We take a two-top by the window. The table is small   small enough that our knees are close under it, and I am intensely aware of the specific geometry of her legs and mine in the space between the table legs and the wall. She shrugs off the olive jacket. The black turtleneck fits closer than I expected   she's small, Kira, genuinely small, 5'3" of compact precision, but the turtleneck shows the line of her shoulders, the slope of her collarbone, the way her neck looks longer without the jacket framing it. She catches me looking and I redirect my gaze to the menu with a speed that probably looks more suspicious than the looking itself.

The menu is handwritten on a wooden board behind the counter. Tonkotsu, shoyu, miso, a vegetarian option that Kira considers and rejects. Prices are printed at the bottom and I scan them with the invisible desperation of a man who needs to find the cheapest thing and order it without making the cheapness obvious.

Tonkotsu: $16. Shoyu: $14. Miso: $15. Sides are $4-8.

I have $47 in my bank account. Minus the $14 for shoyu   the cheapest option   that leaves $33 to survive until... when? The next donation? The next miracle? The $3.50 from this morning doesn't hit my bank for three to five business days, which in financial terms means it doesn't exist.

<<kira "I'm getting the tonkotsu. The TikTok video was specifically about the tonkotsu.">>

<<eli "Shoyu for me.">>

<<kira "You don't want the tonkotsu? It's the whole reason we're here.">>

<<eli "I like shoyu.">>

She gives me a look. It's the look she gives when she knows I'm lying but she's deciding whether to call it out or let me have the dignity of the lie. Her eyes flick down   to the menu, to the prices, back to me. She's doing the math. She knows. She always knows.

<<kira "My treat today.">>

<<eli "No.">>

<<kira "Eli ">>

<<eli "You already drove me here. I can pay for my own ramen.">>

<<kira "I know you can. I'm saying you don't have to.">>

<<eli "Kira.">>

<<kira "It's not charity. It's me wanting to buy my friend lunch because I had a good month and I want to celebrate and I want to celebrate with YOU, specifically, and part of celebrating is not making you do mental math about soup.">>

She says it fast, like she rehearsed it, which means she probably did. Probably planned this   anticipated my resistance, prepared a counter-argument, loaded it and had it ready before we even sat down. That's Kira. Strategic compassion. Kindness with a game plan.

Something in my chest does that thing. The tightening. The same feeling from the coffee shop yesterday when Marcus paid without asking, except this time it's her and the tightening is different   less shame, more... something. A warmth with teeth.

<<eli "...fine. But I'm getting the shoyu.">>

<<kira "Get the tonkotsu.">>

<<eli "Kira.">>

<<kira "Get. The. Tonkotsu. If someone simmers bones for eighteen hours, you respect the bones, Eli.">>

<<eli "...fine.">>

She beams. Not the stream smile. Not the teasing grin. The real one   the one that makes her whole face open up, bright and unguarded, the one that looks like sunrise on a face built for it. I look away because if I keep looking I'm going to say something I can't take back, something that lives in the space between my ribs where all the dangerous feelings have set up camp.

[[Order the ramen|day_2_13]]

:: day_2_13
<<set $day_step to 13>>
She orders for both of us   in Japanese, the first few words, enough to make the chef look up and smile, before switching back to English for the specifics. She speaks Japanese the way she speaks English   quickly, precisely, with a warmth that makes every word sound like it's being offered rather than said.

<<eli "I didn't know you spoke Japanese.">>

<<kira "Just restaurant Japanese and swear words. My grandma would be proud of the first and horrified by the second.">>

The ramen arrives in large, heavy ceramic bowls   the tonkotsu is a cloudy, pale gold, the surface shimmering with pork fat, topped with chashu and a perfectly halved soft-boiled egg and scallions and black garlic oil drizzled in a spiral. It's the most beautiful thing I've seen in a bowl and I include cereal in that category.

I eat. It's   okay, it's incredible. The broth is rich and heavy and silk-smooth, the kind of thing that coats your mouth and sits in your chest like a warm hand. The noodles are springy and perfect. The chashu melts. I haven't eaten something this good in... I genuinely don't know. Months. Maybe longer. My diet has been so uniformly terrible for so long that I'd forgotten food could taste like it was made by someone who gives a shit.

<<kira "Good?">>

<<eli "I'm having a spiritual experience.">>

<<kira "RIGHT? The broth. The BROTH, Eli.">>

<<eli "The bones did not die in vain.">>

She laughs. She's eating too   chopsticks held correctly, the way she does everything, proper and precise   and she's got a small splash of broth on her chin that she doesn't know about. It's a tiny, absurd imperfection on an otherwise perfectly composed face and I want to reach across the table and wipe it away with my thumb and I do not do that. I absolutely do not do that.

<<eli "You've got, uh ">>

I gesture at my own chin. She touches hers. Finds the broth. Her cheeks pink.

<<kira "Oh my god. How long has that been there?">>

<<eli "Only a second.">>

<<kira "You're lying.">>

<<eli "Maybe thirty seconds.">>

<<kira "And you just let me sit here with soup on my face?">>

<<eli "I was weighing whether to tell you or let it become part of your brand.">>

She kicks me under the table. Her foot connects with my shin   gentle, deliberate, the kind of kick that's really a touch disguised as violence. I feel it through my joggers, the brief press of her shoe against my leg. My shin has never been this interesting.

<<kira "You're the worst.">>

<<eli "And yet you bought me ramen.">>

<<kira "A decision I'm reconsidering.">>

She's smiling. The teasing grin. Her foot doesn't fully retreat   it stays close, hovering in the space between our legs under the table, close enough that I can feel its warmth through the air, or maybe that's phantom warmth, the kind your body invents when it wants something to be closer than it is.

We eat. We talk. The conversation flows the way it always does with Kira   not in a straight line but in spirals, each topic orbiting the last, touching and releasing and circling back. She tells me about her morning yoga class (the instructor is "insanely hot and also insanely flexible, which is redundant because you can't be that flexible and NOT be hot"). I tell her about DarkSlayer setting an alarm to watch my stream ("That's actually kind of sweet." "It is NOT sweet." "He's your number one fan, Eli." "He's my number one BULLY." "Same energy."). She tells me about a brand deal she's negotiating with a headphone company ("They want me to say 'these headphones changed my life' and I'm like, they're headphones, they didn't cure my anxiety, Karen."). I tell her about the penguin knee conversation I had with Marcus at 4 AM.

<<kira "Penguins have knees?!">>

<<eli "Hidden inside the body. The whole leg structure is ">>

<<kira "Stop. My worldview.">>

<<eli "The thing you thought was the knee is actually the ankle.">>

<<kira "ELI.">>

<<eli "Their femur is just buried in there. Like an iceberg.">>

<<kira "I am learning things I didn't want to learn in a ramen restaurant.">>

<<eli "Consider it payment for the tonkotsu.">>

She kicks me again under the table. This time her foot doesn't retreat at all. It stays, pressing lightly against the side of my calf. Resting there. Warm through the fabric. I don't move. She doesn't move. We both keep eating and talking and acting like there isn't a point of contact under the table that is burning a hole through every layer of denial I've built over the past eighteen months.

The bowls empty. She insists on finishing her broth by lifting the bowl with both hands and drinking from it directly, which leaves a faint sheen on her upper lip that she wipes with the back of her hand. The gesture is unselfconscious, childish, entirely unlike the composed streamer persona she wears on camera. I file it away in the archive. Kira Facts, Volume 47: drinks broth directly from the bowl. Wipes mouth with hand. Possibly raised by wolves. Gorgeous.

<<kira "So. I have a proposal.">>

<<eli "Okay.">>

<<kira "Come back to my place? I want to show you something.">>

My brain detonates. My face stays neutral through sheer force of will and eight months of practice at hiding emotional responses from a webcam.

<<eli "Show me what?">>

<<kira "My stream setup! I just got new panels and I redid my whole desk layout and I'm weirdly proud of it and none of my friends care about cable management the way you do.">>

<<eli "I don't care about cable management.">>

<<kira "You sent me a forty-five-second voice note last week about how velcro cable ties are superior to zip ties.">>

<<eli "...that was an informational PSA.">>

<<kira "Just come see my setup, you nerd.">>

Her foot is still against my calf. She's looking at me across the table with the teasing grin and the wire-frame glasses and the faintest pink on her cheeks   from the hot ramen, from the warm restaurant, from something else   and her eyes have that quality they get when she's sure of something, when she's decided and the world can either agree or get out of the way.

<<eli "Yeah. Okay.">>

<<kira "Yay!">>

She claps her hands once, quick and bright, and slides out of the booth to go pay. I watch her walk to the counter   the platform sneakers, the black turtleneck, the way she pushes her glasses up while she waits   and I think about the fact that I'm about to be inside her apartment. Her actual apartment. The space where she lives and streams and exists. The space I've only ever seen in the backgrounds of selfies and the occasional stream where she accidentally shows more of the room than she intended.

I'm about to be there. In person. With her. My shin still feels warm where her foot was.
<<set $hint to "Go to Kira's apartment.">>
[[Go to Kira's apartment|neighbourhood]]


:: day_2_14

<img src="images/locations/kira_apartment.jpeg" alt="Kira's Apartment Interior" class="location-image">

Kira's apartment is on the third floor of a building that makes mine look like a condemned structure, which it probably is. Hers has a lobby. A lobby. With a potted plant and a mailbox wall and floor tile that isn't cracked. The elevator works. It's silent. The doors close and we ascend and the absence of mechanical screaming is, to me, a luxury.

<<kira "It's a mess, by the way. I didn't clean.">>

<<eli "Your version of 'mess' and my version of 'mess' are not the same.">>

<<kira "I left a mug on the coffee table.">>

<<eli "I left a microwave burrito wrapper on my counter for two days.">>

<<kira "...okay, we operate on different scales.">>

The elevator opens. Hallway   clean, beige, unremarkable. She walks ahead of me, keys already in hand, and I watch her from behind for exactly 1.5 seconds before redirecting my gaze to the hallway ceiling like it's architecturally fascinating. Her apartment is 3C. She unlocks it, pushes the door open, and steps inside.

<<kira "Welcome to the KiraByte command center.">>

I step in.

It's   okay. It's a one-bedroom. Probably 600 square feet, maybe 650. Not huge. Not lavish. But it's hers in a way that hits me like a physical thing   every surface declares the existence of a person who is here, who is present, who is building something. The living room has a real couch   a grey sectional, small but actual furniture, with throw pillows in pink and black that match the aesthetic of her stream. A coffee table with the aforementioned mug and a stack of manga volumes. A bookshelf with figures and games and a small succulent that looks suspiciously healthy. The kitchen is to the left   a real kitchen, with a real stove and a real fridge that's full-sized and doesn't hum like a dying insect. I can see magnets on it. Photos of Kira with friends, a printout of a J-rock band, a takeout menu from what appears to be a Thai place and then there's the desk.

Her streaming setup is against the far wall, beside the window, and it's   I knew what it looked like from photos. I've seen it in selfie backgrounds and stream clips and the one time she accidentally showed too much of the room on a just-chatting session. But seeing it in person is different. Seeing it in person is the difference between looking at a picture of the ocean and standing at the shoreline.

Dual monitors. Both high-end, clean bezels, mounted on an adjustable arm so they float above the desk surface like mission control screens. A pink-and-black custom mechanical keyboard that probably costs more than my entire setup combined   I can see the artisan keycaps, the coiled cable, the subtle RGB underglow. A high-end webcam perched on top of the main monitor, sleek and modern, the kind that captures 1080p at 60fps and makes a person look like they exist in higher definition than the rest of reality and the ring light   God, the ring light. It's mounted on an articulating arm clamped to the desk edge, a wide circle of LEDs that, even switched off, promises to make whoever sits in its radius look lit from within, luminous, seen.

<img src="images/locations/kira_desk.jpeg" alt="Kira's Desk Setup" class="location-image">

Acoustic foam panels cover the wall behind the desk   but they're not the generic grey rectangles I've seen in setup tours. Hers are arranged in a pattern, alternating black and pink, and mounted between them are anime posters and prints, framed and level, creating a backdrop that's both functional and beautiful. A small shelf above the monitors holds the chibi figurine the viewer sent her   front and center, turned toward the camera position so it's always visible on stream.

This is what success looks like. Not the Lamborghini-and-mansion version. The real version. The version where a person who started from nothing poured enough time and talent and luck into a screen to build something that works, something that sustains, something that has weight.

I look at her setup and I think about mine. The curbside desk with the cardboard wedge. The refurbished monitor and its dead-pixel companion. The 2014 webcam. The budget mic. The absence of everything   no ring light, no foam, no aesthetic, no design, no intention. Just a desk in a dark room. Just a guy talking into a void.

The gap. This is the gap. Not between us as people   I could almost close that distance, almost believe we're equal   but between our realities. Hers is built and lit and functioning. Mine is a sketch on a napkin that someone left in the rain.

<<kira "So? What do you think?">>

She's watching me, leaning against the doorframe of the bedroom with her arms crossed and a half-smile that's looking for approval. She's proud of this. She should be. She built it from nothing   she told me once, during a late-night call, that she started streaming on a laptop with the built-in webcam and a $10 headset from Amazon. Two years ago and now this.

<<eli "It's incredible.">>

<<kira "Yeah?">>

<<eli "The cable management alone is ">>

<<kira "I KNEW you'd notice the cables first!">>

<<eli "They're in braided sleeves, Kira. Braided sleeves. That's not cable management, that's cable couture.">>

She bounces   actually bounces, a little hop of excitement that makes her bangs flutter. Then she grabs my wrist and pulls me toward the desk.

<<kira "Okay, look, look   I just got these new panels, right? The pink ones? and I rearranged the whole layout so the figurine is in frame but the light doesn't cast a shadow on it, and I moved the second monitor so I can see chat without turning my head because I read this article about how eye contact with the camera increases engagement by like 23%">>

She's still holding my wrist. Her hand is small and warm and her thumb is resting against the inside of my wrist, against the pulse point, and I wonder if she can feel my heart rate doing whatever my heart rate is doing right now, which is something between "cardiovascular event" and "percussion solo."

<<eli "You read articles about camera eye contact engagement?">>

<<kira "I'm a professional.">>

<<eli "You're a nerd.">>

<<kira "I'm a nerd who makes rent. Sit.">>

She steers me into her desk chair   a proper gaming chair, black with pink accents, ergonomic, the kind that supports your lower back instead of slowly compressing your spine into dust. I sit. It's comfortable. Offensively comfortable. My desk chair at home is a betrayal of the human skeleton, this is a throne.

<<eli "This chair is better than my bed.">>

<<kira "That says more about your bed than my chair.">>

She leans past me to wake the monitors. Both screens flare to life   her desktop wallpaper is a stylized illustration of a girl with a pixelated sword, the same character as the figurine, her stream mascot. The taskbar is organized. She has folders. Named folders. I'm looking at the desktop of a person who has their shit together, and the desktop of a person who has their shit together looks absolutely nothing like mine.

<<kira "Oh wait, let me show you this ">>

She opens her streaming dashboard. The interface is the same one I use   same platform, same layout   but the numbers in it are from a different planet. A different galaxy. A different genre of existence.

[[Check dashboard|day_2_15]]

:: day_2_15
<<set $day_step = 15>>
She tabs to last night's stream.

KiraByte   VOD: Just Chatting + Gunmetal ft. Chat Challenge
Duration: 4 hours 12 minutes
Peak Viewers: 1,387
Average Viewers: 1,108
New Followers: 89
New Subscribers: 34
Donations: $487.23
Top Donation: $50 from WhaleKing_420   "kira ur the best streamer on the platform never stop"

I look at those numbers.

Eleven hundred average viewers. Eighty-nine new followers. Thirty-four subscribers. Four hundred and eighty-seven dollars. In one session. In four hours. She sat at this desk, in this chair, in front of this ring light, and four hours later she had earned more than I've earned in eight months of streaming. My total lifetime earnings   all of it, every donation, every cent since I started EliPlays   is somewhere around $180. She made nearly triple that in a single Tuesday evening.

The gap isn't a gap. It's a canyon. It's the Grand Canyon. It's the Mariana Trench turned on its side.

<<kira "It was a good night. Chat was really active. We did this thing where I let them pick my loadout in Gunmetal and I had to play with whatever they chose, and they gave me the worst possible combination   the starter pistol and a smoke grenade   and I somehow clutched a round and the whole chat went crazy.">>

<<eli "Fourteen tho peak viewers.">>

<<kira "Yeah! That was during the clutch. It got clipped and shared on Twitter. Or X. Whatever it's called now.">>

<<eli "Four hundred and eighty-seven dollars.">>

<<kira "That's actually below my average for a four-hour session. Last week I hit six-twenty.">>

Six hundred and twenty dollars. For one stream. My mouth is dry. I'm sitting in her chair, in front of her monitors, staring at her numbers, and my mouth is dry and my hands are on my knees and I can feel the disparity like a temperature   cold, creeping, the specific chill of looking at proof that the same activity you do can produce wildly different results depending on variables you don't control.

<<eli "How.">>

It comes out flat. Not hostile. Just... flat. A genuine question stripped of everything except the need to know.

<<kira "Honestly?">>

<<eli "Yeah.">>

She pulls up her desk chair   a separate one, smaller, that she keeps in the corner for when friends visit   and sits beside me. Close. Our knees are almost touching. She smells like cherry blossom and ramen broth and something underneath both that's just skin and warmth.

<<kira "It's not one thing. It's a bunch of things. My setup   yes. My content strategy   yeah, I think about it. My schedule, my consistency, my community management. I reply to DMs. I remember regulars' names. I do gift sub trains. I have emotes that people actually use.">>

She pauses. Takes her glasses off. Cleans them on the hem of her turtleneck. Without the glasses her face looks different   younger, softer, more exposed. Like the glasses are a frame and without them she's an unframed painting, beautiful but vulnerable.

<<kira "But if I'm being honest with you   really honest   the biggest reason is this.">>

She gestures at herself. At her face. At her body. At the whole of her, sitting there in the desk chair.

<<kira "I'm a girl. I'm a cute girl on camera. That matters more than any strategy or content plan or cable management in the world.">>

<<eli "Kira ">>

<<kira "No, listen. I'm not saying I don't work hard. I work my ass off. But I've seen guys who work just as hard   harder   and they're stuck at 50 viewers because the algorithm doesn't surface them the same way, and the audience doesn't click on them the same way, and the donation culture around male streamers is completely different. It's not fair. I know it's not fair. But it's real.">>

She puts her glasses back on. Looks at me directly.

<<kira "I get donations because I'm pretty. I get subscribers because guys want to feel close to a girl who pays attention to them. I get viewers because when a cute face shows up on a thumbnail, people click. The content keeps them there   I'm good at what I do, I know that   but the click? The first click? That's because I look like this.">>

She gestures at her face again. The wire-frame glasses. The blunt bangs. The sharp, intelligent eyes.

<<kira "If I were a guy with the exact same personality, the exact same humor, the exact same stream quality   I'd be you, Eli. I'd be at six viewers with DarkSlayer telling me I suck.">>

The silence between us is loud. The apartment hums with the low noise of her PC fans. Outside, a car passes. Inside, I'm sitting in front of a dashboard that shows $487 and thinking about my $3.50 and the gap between those numbers is shaped like a face   specifically, like the difference between her face and mine.

<<eli "So it's just... looks.">>

<<kira "Not just. But mostly.">>

<<eli "That's depressing.">>

<<kira "It's capitalism.">>

<<eli "Same thing.">>

She laughs. Sad, short. Not her real laugh   the muted one, the one that carries weight.

<<kira "I know it's shitty to hear. I've thought about whether to tell you for a while. But you keep asking me how to grow and I keep giving you advice about titles and schedules and I ">>

She stops. Her jaw tightens. That thing she does when she's deciding something.

<<kira "The advice isn't wrong. But it's incomplete. The complete answer includes the fact that the streaming economy rewards a specific thing, and that thing is   and I hate saying this   an attractive girl on camera. It's the reality. I didn't make the rules. But I'd be lying to you if I didn't tell you they exist.">>

[[Accept the truth|day_2_16]]

:: day_2_16
<<set $day_step to 16>>
I nod. I don't know what to say. Every stream tip video I've watched, every forum post I've read, every "how to grow on Twitch" guide   they all dance around this. Be consistent. Engage with chat. Find your niche. Never: Be born with a face people want to look at. Preferably female. Because you can't put that in a YouTube thumbnail. You can't monetize that truth.

But Kira just said it. To my face. In her apartment, in front of her $487 dashboard, she looked at me and told me the truth that every small male streamer knows and nobody says.

<<eli "I appreciate you being honest.">>

<<kira "I'd rather you hate me for being honest than like me for being nice.">>

<<eli "I could never hate you.">>

It comes out too soft. Too much. I hear it leave my mouth and I know it's too much because her eyes change   something shifts behind the glasses, a flicker, a warmth, and she uncrosses her legs and recrosses them and looks away for a second before looking back.

<<kira "Do you want to stream together? Just for fun. No pressure. I can host you on my channel for a bit   it won't be a real collab, just me introducing you to my chat. It might help.">>

I should say no. Her chat seeing me   seeing my face, my setup, my everything   beside her would be a study in contrast. The before-and-after. The success and the failure, side by side. But she's offering something and I can hear in her voice that the offering matters to her, that she planned this too, the way she planned the ramen and the pickup and the "my treat," and saying no would be rejecting not just the stream but the intention behind it.

<<eli "Yeah. Okay. Let's do it.">>

[[Stream with Kira|day_2_17]]

:: day_2_17
<<set $day_step to 17>>
<<set $hint to "Stream with Kira.">>
She sets up a Discord call between our accounts and adds my webcam feed as a secondary overlay on her stream layout. My face appears in a small rectangle in the upper right corner of her screen   grainy, poorly lit, transmitted through my phone's front camera because my actual webcam is at home. I look like a hostage video next to her professionally lit, ring-light-enhanced, 1080p60 perfection.

<<kira "Ready?">>

<<eli "Define 'ready.'">>

<<kira "Able to speak words into a microphone without having a panic attack.">>

<<eli "...conditionally.">>

She goes live. The viewer count jumps   not gradually, not the slow trickle I'm used to, but a surge. 200. 400. 600. In under a minute. Her community floods in, the chat scrolling so fast it's almost unreadable, a cascade of emotes and greetings and inside jokes I don't understand.

<<chat "kira_chat_mod_1" "KIRAAAA">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_2" "hi kira!! 💕">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_3" "queen is live lets goooo">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_4" "who's the guy in the corner?">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_5" "wait is that a dude on stream?">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_6" "kira has a boyfriend??? 😢">>

<<kira "Hey everyone! Happy Thursday! So today I have a special guest   my friend Eli! He streams on his own channel, EliPlays, he's super funny, say hi, Eli.">>

<<eli "Uh, hey everyone.">>

My voice sounds like a dial tone next to hers. She's projecting   bright, warm, the streamer voice, the one that fills a room and makes you feel like you're the only person listening even when 800 other people are. Mine is flat, hesitant, the voice of a guy who's used to talking to four viewers and a bot.

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_7" "hi eli">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_8" "bro looks tired lol">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_9" "is this the friend she talks about sometimes?">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_10" "no offense but why is his cam quality so bad 😂">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_11" "are they dating??">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_12" "he's kinda mid ngl">>

 <<chat "kira_chat_mod_1" "be nice chat or timeout hammer comes out">>

She glances at the chat. I can see her reading the messages. Her jaw tightens for a fraction of a second   she saw the "mid" comment, the camera quality joke   and then she's back, professional, redirecting.

<<kira "Eli and I are going to do some duo Gunmetal! We're going to see if we can carry each other. Fair warning   we're both terrible.">>

<<eli "Speak for yourself. I'm strategically terrible.">>

A few laughs in chat. Not many. But a few.

[[Continue streaming|day_2_18]]

:: day_2_18
<<set $day_step to 18>>
<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_13" "lol strategically terrible">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_14" "give him a chance chat hes funny">>

We play. She's better than me   she'd have to be, at her viewer level, because the audience expects competence   but she's not dramatically better. She dies to a flanker and screams and I hear the scream through my phone and through her monitors simultaneously, a half-second delay that creates a brief, disorienting echo. I clutch a round, an actual clutch, a 1v3 that involves a gadget trick I didn't know I could pull off, and for ten seconds the chat scrolls with 

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_15" "WAIT">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_16" "HE POPPED OFF">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_17" "ok eli that was clean">>

<<chat "kira_chat_viewer_18" "eli cracked??">>

<<kira "ELI! THAT WAS INSANE! Did you guys see that?!">>

She's yelling. Genuinely. Her voice is high and bright and she's clapping and her chat is reacting and for ten seconds   ten perfect, unreal seconds   I feel it. The thing I've been chasing for eight months. Attention. Recognition. The warmth of being seen by more than four people and a troll.

Then the round ends. The next one starts. I die immediately. The chat moves on. By the third round they've forgotten the clutch and they're back to talking about Kira   her outfit, her new panels, the figurine, an inside joke about something that happened last stream. My webcam feed sits in the upper right corner of the screen like a footnote, a small grey rectangle that the chat glances at occasionally the way you glance at a clock   not because it's interesting, but because it's there.

Viewer count during our play session: Kira's average sits at 940. My channel, which I linked in her chat, gets 3 new clicks. Three. Out of 940 people who could have clicked, three did.

We play for forty-five minutes. She's entertaining throughout   reacting, bantering, engaging chat, doing bits. I try. I contribute. I make jokes that land about 30% of the time and fall flat the other 70%. When I speak, chat occasionally acknowledges me. When she speaks, chat explodes. The asymmetry is constant, visible, measurable.

After the session she raids me. All 940 viewers, dumped into my channel at once. My viewer count   which was at zero, because I'm not actually streaming on my own channel   spikes to 940 for exactly twelve seconds before the raid disperses. 940 to 12. The number crashes like a stock after a pump-and-dump. Within two minutes, I'm back to nothing.

[[End the stream|day_2_19]]

:: day_2_19
<<set $day_step to 19>>
She ends the stream. The monitors go quiet. The ring light powers down and the room shifts from professional to personal, the warm afternoon light replacing the cold LED glow.

<<kira "That was fun! You did great.">>

<<eli "I got three clicks, Kira.">>

<<kira "It takes time.">>

<<eli "You had nine hundred and forty viewers and three of them clicked on my channel. That's 0.3 percent. I have a lower conversion rate than spam email.">>

<<kira "Eli ">>

<<eli "I'm not mad. I'm just... doing math.">>

She's quiet. She knows the math. She did the math before I did   she's been doing it since before we met, since she first started climbing and noticed the guys around her staying flat. She knows what those numbers mean. She knows what they say about me. About my face, my voice, my everything.

I stand up from her chair. Cross to the couch. Sit down. The cushions are real cushions, supportive, the kind that hold you rather than consume you. Everything in this apartment holds. Everything supports. Everything works.

She stays at the desk for a moment, watching me, and then she follows. Sits in her desk chair, rolls it toward the couch, stops about three feet away. Close enough to talk. Far enough to give me space.

<<kira "I know it's frustrating.">>

<<eli "It's not frustrating. It's   it's physics. It's gravity. You can't fight gravity. You just fall.">>

<<kira "You're not falling.">>

<<eli "I'm not flying either.">>

She leans forward. Her elbows on her knees. The afternoon light from the window hits her from the side, catching the wire-frame glasses, the edge of her jawline, the small gold hoops in her ears. The pixel-heart tattoo on her inner wrist is visible as she clasps her hands, the black ink vivid against her fair skin. She's mid-sentence about something   algorithmic bias, recommendation engines, the way the platform surfaces content based on viewer retention metrics and how thumbnail click-through rates are disproportionately influenced by 

I'm staring.

Not at the dashboard. Not at the monitors. Not at the ring light or the acoustic panels or the braided cable sleeves.

At her.

The way the light catches the edge of her glasses. The way her bangs sit against her forehead, perfectly straight, a geometric precision that frames the soft chaos of her face when she's passionate about something. The way her lips move when she talks fast   slightly ahead of the words, like her mouth is racing to keep up with her brain. The pixel-heart tattoo, the tiny permanent declaration of identity inked into her skin, visible because she gestures when she speaks, her hands painting the air between us.

She catches me.

<<kira "...and the click-through rate on thumbnails with  are you listening?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Sorry. You just... you're really smart.">>

It's not smooth. It's not flirtatious. It's me   awkward, sincere, accidentally honest. The kind of honesty that happens when your mouth outruns the part of your brain responsible for self-preservation.

Kira blinks. A faint pink crosses her cheeks   not the ramen flush or the cold flush but something new, something underneath, something warm blooming from below the surface. She pushes her glasses up. The nervous gesture. The tell.

<<kira "I was literally talking about click-through rates.">>

<<eli "I know. It's hot.">>

I say it before my brain catches up. The word leaves my mouth and hangs in the air between us like a flare, red and impossible to ignore, illuminating everything. My face goes hot. Not warm   hot. The kind of heat that starts at the neck and climbs and there's nowhere to hide from it because I'm sitting on her couch in her apartment three feet from her face and I just called her hot.

Her face goes red too. Not faintly   fully. The pink bloom rises past her cheeks and reaches her ears and the bridge of her nose. Behind the wire-frame glasses, her eyes widen by a fraction. Her lips part. She uncrosses her legs.

The room vibrates. I don't mean that metaphorically   I mean the air between us feels different, charged, like the moment before a storm when the pressure drops and everything goes quiet and electric simultaneously. The PC fans hum. Outside, a siren passes, distant and irrelevant. Inside, the space between the couch and her desk chair is three feet of charged atmosphere that neither of us is crossing and neither of us is retreating from.

She looks at me. I look at her. Three seconds of silence. They feel like thirty.

<span style="color: #ff69b4; font-weight: bold;">Kira's eyes are wide. Her cheeks are pink. Her lips are parted. She's looking at me like she just realized something important, something that was there all along but she never noticed until this moment.</span>

<span style="color:rgb(144, 7, 7); font-weight: bold;">**THIS IS MAJOR BRANCHING POINT</span>

[[Laugh it off|day_2_20_other]] | [[Hold her gaze|day_2_20_kira]]